Showing Function Output
By default, if a test passes, Rust’s test library captures anything printed to standard output. For
example, if we call println! in a test and the test passes, we won’t see the println! output
in the terminal; we’ll see only the line that indicates the test passed. If a test fails, we’ll see
whatever was printed to standard output with the rest of the failure message.
As an example, Listing 11-10 has a silly function that prints the value of its parameter and
returns 10, as well as a test that passes and a test that fails.
